</code></pre> <p>(Test files run in parallel, so the order of printed lines interleaves by file.) What each group proved:</p> <ul> <li>Domain: the September expense was excluded from October; totals, over-budget state and category ordering are right; months   wrap across years; input parsing accepts <code>12,5</code> (comma decimal) but rejects three decimals, negatives and text; <code>intl</code> formats   <code>$1,234.56</code> and <code>1.234,56 €</code> correctly per locale.</li> <li>Repository: the on-disk JSON is exactly what we intended and a future file version is refused.</li> <li>App flows: the month view's numbers match the seed (300.00 + 64.20 + 2.50 = 366.70); September shows only the cinema ticket;   invalid input is rejected, a valid add updates the budget and saves once; editing groceries from 64.20 to 80 changed the total   by 15.80; swipe-delete and Undo round-trip (three saves: edit, delete, undo).</li> <li>Failure: with a repository whose save throws, the deletion was rolled back — the total still includes the rent.</li> <li>Robustness: a deep link to a nonexistent expense shows a friendly page.</li> <li>Accessibility: tap-target, label and contrast guidelines pass on the month page, and the chart reads as a sentence.</li> <li>Architecture: no layer imports what it mustn't.</li> </ul> <p>The golden test locks the month page's layout (the test font draws text as boxes; colours and geometry are real):</p> <p></p> <p>The "DEBUG" ribbon in the corner is <code>MaterialApp</code>'s debug banner; set <code>debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false</code> if you'd rather keep it out of goldens.</p>
